; midi_xfer.pe — a MIDI 1.0 transmitter in software, at 31.25 kbaud, using
; RUNNING STATUS.
;
; The first half of the rate-flexibility act, and the reason it is not
; uart_echo.pe with a different constant is the timing: at 31.25 kbaud a bit is
; 32 us and the SoC's shared tick is 4.3333 us, so a bit is 7.38 ticks. The
; tick cannot express a fraction. This program builds the bit cell out of
; WHOLE INSTRUCTIONS, and that is the piece wiki/plans/through-i2c.md records
; as a known follow-up ("removing it needs a sub-tick delay, roughly 130
; clocks of counted NOPs").
;
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; TWO DEFECTS THIS FILE HAD, BOTH FOUND BY MEASURING THE PIN
;
; Both were found by the testbench, and both are recorded here because the
; arithmetic in the old header was confidently WRONG in a way only a
; measurement could catch. Neither is a subtle bit-order slip: both would
; decode as SOMETHING under a forgiving receiver, which is the worst shape a
; bug can have.
;
; (1) THE RATE WAS TWICE THE MIDI RATE. The old header said "half-bit at
;     31.25 kbaud = 960 clocks = 16.000 us", which is true, and then built
;     960 clocks PER BIT CELL. A cell is a whole bit, not a half: 16.316 us
;     measured on the pin is 61,286 baud, not 31,250. The old header went on
;     to report "31,188 baud, 0.2% from nominal" -- a number produced by
;     dividing the measured cell by two, i.e. by asking the wrong question of
;     the right measurement. The testbench's rate window caught it only
;     because it is anchored to 32.000 us and not to the firmware's own
;     arithmetic.
;
; (2) EVERY BYTE LOST ITS TWO LOWEST BITS. The delay, the shift and the cell
;     index advance all lived in sb_hold, the common tail, so the STOP cell
;     and the START cell shifted the working copy as well. Ten cells therefore
;     shifted ten times for eight bits, and what went on the wire was the
;     source byte shifted right by two: 0x90 arrived as 0x24, 0x20 as 0x08,
;     0x40 as 0x10, on all fourteen frames. Only the two highest bits of every
;     MIDI status byte survive that, so a receiver would have decoded six
;     well-formed messages built out of the wrong data. The shift now lives
;     in the DATA branch, and the rule is: a cell that does not carry a data
;     bit must not consume one.
;
; There was also a third, smaller one: the prologue drove the raw byte on the
; pin, so for any byte with bit 0 clear (0x90, 0x20, 0x40 -- half of MIDI's
; range) the line went low for ~150 ns on an idle-HIGH line between bytes. A
; real receiver may read that as a false start. The prologue now drives the
; idle level it means, which costs one instruction outside the timed cells.
;
; (3) THERE WAS NO STOP BIT AT THE END OF THE FRAME, and this one is the
;     argument for the receiver in tb_pe_soc_midi.v. The cell dispatch tested
;     the index for ZERO first, so cell 0 drove 1 and cell 1 drove 0: the wire
;     carried the cells in the order [stop][start][d0..d7], one cell out of
;     phase, and the only high cell in a frame was the NEXT frame's first one.
;     A back-to-back stream is therefore perfectly well formed -- the missing
;     stop bit is the next frame's leading cell -- and the receiver decoded
;     THIRTEEN OF THE FOURTEEN BYTES CORRECTLY, including the 0x90 and the
;     running-status flip to 0x80. It failed only on the last frame, which
;     has no following frame to supply a stop bit, and the way it failed was a
;     single stop sample reading low.
;
;     So the byte-comparison checks did not find this and the rate check did
;     not either. The one check that did is the receiver's: a frame is
;     ACCEPTED ONLY IF ITS STOP BIT READS HIGH, and a candidate whose stop
;     reads low is discarded. That is the whole reason the receiver is
;     written as an oversampling search with a verified stop bit rather than
;     as a position counter, and it is worth writing down that a 13-of-14
;     pass is a failure, not a near-miss.
;
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE TIMING, AND WHERE EVERY NUMBER COMES FROM
;
;   1 clock                  16.667 ns           (60 MHz, rtl/pe_soc.v)
;   bit at 31.25 kbaud       1920 clocks = 32.000 us
;   -> the whole thing, counted instructions
;
; ONE INSTRUCTION IS ONE CLOCK IS MEASURED, NOT ASSUMED. The old version of
; this loop counted 979 instructions from one cell's OUT to the next and a
; clock-resolution probe of the TX pin measured 979 clocks (16.316 us). That
; exact agreement is what makes the arithmetic below load-bearing rather than
; decorative, and it is the single fact the whole file rests on.
;
; TWO NOPs THEN THE LDI IS THE FINE ADJUSTMENT, and the loop count is the
; coarse one. The cell is
;
;   22  the code around the loop, counted from this cell's OUT to the next
;    1  the LDI that loads the loop count
;   146  iterations of a 13-clock body
;   ---
;  1920 clocks = 32.000 us = 31,250 baud, 0.00% error.
;
; The body is ELEVEN NOPs plus SUB and JNZ, and 13 is not a round choice: the
; delay has to be 1898 clocks (1920 less the 23 around it), and 1898 = 13 x 146
; is the only factorisation of it whose body is small enough to write out and
; whose count fits an 8-bit register.
;
; KEEP EVERY DELAY-LOOP COUNTER <= 255, and this is not a style rule. The
; obvious alternative here is a 3-clock body, which wants 1900/3 = 634
; iterations: an 8-bit register cannot hold that, the assembler does not
; complain, and the loop silently runs 634 mod 256 = 122 iterations and
; produces a bit period of 20% of the intended one. A previous version of this
; file did exactly that with a 319-iteration loop, measured "the delay", and
; reported the time since reset as the result. A delay that is measured only
; against a value the firmware itself computes cannot catch this.
;
; A CELL'S LENGTH BELONGS TO THE PATH BETWEEN TWO CELLS, and this is the
; single most expensive piece of knowledge in the file, because it is the one
; that is invisible until you measure the wire cell by cell.
;
; The obvious model is "each cell drives its bit, waits, and repeats", and
; under that model the padding NOPs below look like arbitrary constants. They
; are not: the time from one cell's OUTPUT to the next cell's OUTPUT depends on
; WHICH CELL IS NEXT, because the dispatch that chooses the next cell's code
; is only partly executed on each path. Reaching the stop branch runs three
; instructions of the dispatch; reaching the start branch or the data branch
; runs five, and then the start branch's own body is one instruction shorter
; than the data branch's. So the cells in a frame measure:
;
;   cell 0  start   1920   after three NOPs in sb_start
;   cells 1-7 data   1920   the reference: no padding anywhere
;   cell 8  d7      1917   WITHOUT the three NOPs at the top of sb_stop,
;                          because the last data bit's destination is the
;                          stop and that dispatch is the short one
;   cell 9  stop    1920 + the message layer's inter-frame gap
;
; and cell 0 came out at 1921 with four NOPs instead of three. All of that is
; measured, at clock resolution, on the pin -- an intermediate version of this
; file put cells of 1921, 1920 and 1917 in the same frame and the testbench
; reported a 32.0086 us cell with a 0.9-clock spread, which is a rate error
; with no obvious source. A receiver tolerates it; an assertion about the rate
; does not, and the whole point of this act is the assertion.
;
; So the padding is eleven NOPs in total, all of which exist only to make some
; OTHER cell's duration come out at 1920. That is the honest cost of a bit
; banged protocol in an ISA with no timer worth using, and it is also the
; argument for the 0.3% rate window in the testbench: the transmitter is
; exact to the clock, so the window can be tight enough to see a one-iteration
; change in the loop count.
;
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; WHY THE TIMER IS NOT USED AT ALL, and this is the most useful thing here.
;
; The obvious construction is a few whole ticks plus a remainder, and it does
; not work. pe_soc's target idiom -- read the counter, add N, poll until it
; equals the target -- does NOT give N ticks: it gives anywhere in (N-1, N]
; ticks, for exactly the reason firmware/uart_echo.pe's header records for the
; 1-tick case ("a wait that snapshots the count and loops until it changes
; therefore returns after anywhere in (0, 1] ticks"). The read lands at a
; random phase within the counter's 260-clock window, so a 3-tick wait is
; anywhere in (520, 780] clocks -- a 260-clock spread, which on a 1920-clock
; cell is 13.5%.
;
; That is survivable at 115200, where a receiver re-synchronises on every
; start bit and the jitter is 1.2% of a bit. It is NOT survivable here: an
; earlier version built the cell from three whole ticks plus a remainder, the
; sampling grid drifted, and after eight bits it was a third of a cell away
; from where it started -- which is why that run decoded 0x20 and 0x40
; correctly and 0x21 as 0x10. A UART cannot fix a transmitter that jitters;
; only a deterministic delay can. So MIDI, like DMX, is timed from counted
; instructions alone, and the consequence worth recording is that this act is
; really about the TIMER's granularity, not about MIDI.
;
; The testbench MEASURES the bit period on the pin INSIDE each frame, between
; two edges that are a whole number of cells apart and therefore immune to
; the message layer's inter-frame gap, and checks it against a window narrower
; than the delay loop's own quantisation. So none of the arithmetic above is
; load-bearing on faith, and a one-iteration change to the count (0.678%)
; falls outside that window -- which is what makes the mutation in
; regress/mutate_fwbus_tb.sh a real test rather than a formality.
;
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE MESSAGES, AND WHAT RUNNING STATUS IS FOR
;
; Six two-data-byte messages on channel 0, fourteen bytes on the wire:
;
;   m  status  note  vel
;   0  0x90    0x20   0x40     the status byte IS sent
;   1  --      0x21   0x41     running status 0x90
;   2  --      0x22   0x42     running status 0x90
;   3  0x80    0x23   0x43     a NEW status byte: note-off, and it REPLACES
;   4  --      0x24   0x44     the running status
;   5  --      0x25   0x45     running status 0x80
;
; Six messages cost eighteen bytes if every one repeats its status byte.
; Running status is what makes it fourteen, and that saving is the point of the
; feature: a 31.25 kbaud wire saturates near 3,900 bytes per second and the
; status bytes are a fifth of them.
;
; THE PART THAT IS EASY TO GET WRONG, and which the testbench checks: a new
; status byte REPLACES the running status rather than adding to it. A receiver
; that kept 0x90 after seeing 0x80 would decode messages 4 and 5 as note-ONS.
; The firmware sends 0x80 exactly once, at m=3, and the testbench's expected
; event list puts messages 4-6 under 0x80 -- so a receiver with the wrong rule
; reconstructs three different messages and fails.
;
; Every data byte is < 0x80, which is what makes the wire self-delimiting: a
; receiver decides "status or data" from the top bit alone. (MIDI 1.0 confines
; data bytes to 0x00-0x7F; the escape for a larger value is the 0xF7 prefix,
; which this firmware never needs because every value it sends fits.)
;
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; dmem IS THE OBSERVABLE INTERFACE
;
;   dmem[0]  the byte being shifted out (the working copy)
;   dmem[1]  the message counter, 0..5
;   dmem[2]  the mod-3 counter that decides where a status byte goes
;   dmem[3]  the current status byte
;   dmem[4]  bytes transmitted
;   dmem[5]  STATUS bytes transmitted -- the running-status claim, made
;            observable. The testbench requires 2 against 6 messages, so a
;            firmware that repeated the status fails on the COUNT and not
;            merely on a byte value.
;   dmem[6]  bit cells remaining in the frame (10: start + 8 data + stop)
;   dmem[7]  the cell index, 0 = start bit, 9 = stop bit
;   dmem[8]  scratch (it held the tick target until the half-bit became
;            counted-only -- see the header for why the timer is not usable)
;   dmem[9]  scratch
;   dmem[10] scratch
;   dmem[11] 0xA5 once all six messages are out
;   dmem[12] the byte the message layer hands to the transmitter
;   dmem[13] the transmitter's RETURN POINT: 0 = next message, 1 = the note,
;            2 = the velocity. The ISA has no CALL/RET, so a shared
;            transmitter gets an explicit continuation rather than being
;            written out three times.
;   dmem[14] scratch
;   dmem[15] scratch

        LDI   A, 1               ; TX idle is HIGH: stated, not inherited
        OUT   TXPIN, A
        LDI   A, 0
        STM   1, A
        STM   2, A
        STM   3, A
        STM   4, A
        STM   5, A
        STM   11, A

; ===========================================================================
; The message layer decides WHAT goes on the wire. The transmitter below only
; knows how to put a byte out at 31.25 kbaud.
; ===========================================================================
main:   LDM   A, 1
        SUB   A, 6
        JZ    done
        ; ---- does this message carry a status byte? -------------------
        ; A mod-3 counter rather than a division: the ISA has neither, and
        ; that is why DECX exists. Status bytes fall on m = 0 and m = 3.
        LDM   A, 2
        JNZ   go_note
        ; The status byte, and the reason it is sent at all: the direction
        ; flips at m=3 and a receiver may not assume it, so the wire has to
        ; say so. It is only ever sent at those two points, so the same
        ; comparison that finds the point decides the byte.
        LDM   A, 1
        JZ    stat_on
        LDI   A, 0x80
        JMP   stat_go
stat_on:
        LDI   A, 0x90
stat_go:
        STM   3, A
        STM   12, A
        LDM   A, 5               ; the running-status claim, made observable
        ADD   A, 1
        STM   5, A               ; raised HERE, not in the transmitter: the
        LDI   A, 1               ; transmitter cannot tell a status byte
        STM   13, A              ; from a data byte, and must not guess
        JMP   send_byte

; ---- the note ----------------------------------------------------------
go_note:
        LDM   A, 1
        ADD   A, 0x20            ; note = 0x20 + m
        STM   12, A
        LDI   A, 2               ; return: the velocity follows
        STM   13, A
        JMP   send_byte

; ---- the velocity ------------------------------------------------------
go_vel:
        LDM   A, 1
        ADD   A, 0x40            ; velocity = 0x40 + m
        STM   12, A
        LDI   A, 0               ; return: the message is complete
        STM   13, A
        JMP   send_byte

; ---- advance the message and the mod-3 counter ------------------------
next_msg:
        LDM   A, 2
        ADD   A, 1
        SUB   A, 3
        JZ    mod3_zero
        LDM   A, 2
        ADD   A, 1
        STM   2, A
        JMP   msg_adv
mod3_zero:
        LDI   A, 0
        STM   2, A
msg_adv:
        LDM   A, 1
        ADD   A, 1
        STM   1, A
        JMP   main

done:   LDI   A, 0xA5
        STM   11, A
park:   JMP   park

; ===========================================================================
; The transmitter: 31.25 kbaud, 8N1, LSB first, as ONE loop over the ten bit
; cells of a frame.
;
; ONE LOOP RATHER THAN A SUBROUTINE, and that is a deliberate consequence of
; the ISA. There is no CALL/RET, so a shared cell routine would need a
; return address in a dmem slot and a dispatch to get back -- more state, and
; one more thing to get wrong, in exchange for nothing. Ten cells in a loop is
; also how the wire is actually shaped, so the code reads as the frame does.
;
; `OUT TXPIN, A` with A = 0 or 1 drives the WHOLE byte, so bit 0 is TX and the
; rest of the port is don't-care here. That is the same shared 8-bit port
; uart_echo.pe, i2c_xfer.pe and spi_mode3.pe use: nothing in the RTL knows
; which protocol is running.
; ===========================================================================
send_byte:
        LDM   A, 12
        STM   0, A               ; the working copy: the WHOLE byte, and the
        LDI   A, 1               ; only thing that ever shifts it
        OUT   TXPIN, A           ; the line idles HIGH between bytes
        LDI   A, 10
        STM   6, A               ; ten bit cells
        LDI   A, 0
        STM   7, A               ; cell 0

sbit:   LDM   A, 7
        SUB   A, 9
        JZ    sb_stop            ; cell 9 is the stop bit
        ADD   A, 9               ; eight-bit arithmetic wraps, so this is
        JZ    sb_start           ; the index again -- but see below
        LDM   A, 0
        AND   A, 1               ; a data bit, and the byte goes LSB first
        OUT   TXPIN, A
        ; ---- THE SHIFT LIVES HERE AND NOT IN sb_hold -------------------
        ; A cell that carries no data bit must not consume one. With the
        ; shift in the common tail the stop and start cells each shifted as
        ; well, ten shifts for eight bits, and every byte arrived on the wire
        ; shifted right by two -- see the header's second defect. Moving three
        ; instructions out of the shared path is what the padding NOPs below
        ; are paying for.
        LDM   A, 0
        SHR   A
        STM   0, A
        JMP   sb_hold
sb_start:
        LDI   A, 0
        OUT   TXPIN, A
        NOP                      ; three NOPs, and their number is not a
        NOP                      ; matter of taste. See the header, "A CELL'S
        NOP                      ;  LENGTH BELONGS TO THE PATH BETWEEN TWO
        JMP   sb_hold            ;  CELLS": this branch's dispatch to a DATA
sb_stop:                         ;  cell is two instructions longer than the
        NOP                      ;  data branch's own head, and these make up
        NOP                      ;  the difference.
        NOP
        LDI   A, 1
        OUT   TXPIN, A
        NOP                      ; five more, AFTER the output this time: the
        NOP                      ; stop cell's own length is the path from its
        NOP                      ; output to the next frame's start bit, and
        NOP                      ; the stop branch's dispatch to a start is
        NOP                      ; one instruction shorter than to a data cell

; ---- the bit cell: THE PIECE THIS ACT EXISTS FOR ----------------------
;
; ELEVEN NOPs THEN SUB AND JNZ IS NOT AROUNDING. It is 146 iterations of a
; 13-clock body, and 13 is what makes 1920 fit an 8-bit counter (see the
; header's factorisation). The first version of this file used three whole
; timer ticks plus a 182-clock remainder -- the shape every other firmware
; here uses -- and it produced a half-bit anywhere in (952, 1212] clocks,
; enough jitter that the testbench's sampling grid walked out of its cell and
; the payload decoded wrong from the third message on.
sb_hold:
        NOP
        NOP
        LDI   A, 146             ; 22 + 13*146 = 1920 clocks, which is
sb_rem: NOP                     ; 32.000 us and exactly 31,250 baud
        NOP
        NOP
        NOP
        NOP
        NOP
        NOP
        NOP
        NOP
        NOP
        NOP
        SUB   A, 1
        JNZ   sb_rem
        ; ---- advance to the next cell -----------------------------------
        LDM   A, 7
        ADD   A, 1
        STM   7, A
        LDM   A, 6
        SUB   A, 1
        STM   6, A
        JNZ   sbit
        ; ---- the frame is out ------------------------------------------
        LDM   A, 4
        ADD   A, 1
        STM   4, A               ; bytes transmitted
        ; back to the message layer, at the point it asked for
        LDM   A, 13
        JZ    next_msg
        SUB   A, 1
        JZ    go_note
        JMP   go_vel
